# .claudeignore — paths AI coding tools should NOT read or index.
#
# Why this file exists:
#   1. Context quality — an agent's context window is finite; feeding it lockfiles,
#      build output, or dependency directories crowds out the code that matters and
#      degrades its output. Point agents at source, not at what's generated from it.
#   2. Secrets — .env files must never enter an AI context window. Ignoring them
#      here is defense in depth on top of .gitignore.
#   3. Cost/speed — huge generated files burn tokens and time for zero insight.
#
# How it's enforced: gitignore-style patterns. Tool support varies — some AI tools
# honor ignore files natively; for Claude Code, hard read-protection belongs in
# .claude/settings.json permission rules. Treat this file as the tool-agnostic
# declaration of what agents have no business reading.
#
# CUSTOMIZE: prune entries that cannot apply to your stack (e.g. Python artifacts
# in a JS-only repo) and add your project's own generated/secret paths.

# Build and runtime
.next/
dist/
build/
out/
node_modules/
__pycache__/
*.pyc
target/

# Agent worktrees — full copies of the repository (Claude Code creates them under .claude/worktrees/).
# Searching them returns stale duplicates of every file. Also add this path to .gitignore.
.claude/worktrees/

# Test artifacts
test-results/
playwright-report/
coverage/
.nyc_output/

# Environment and secrets
.env
.env.local
.env.*.local

# IDE
.vscode/
.idea/
*.swp
*.swo

# OS
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db

# Package manager
package-lock.json
yarn.lock
pnpm-lock.yaml

# Docker volumes
data/
postgres-data/
